WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.440
It's often distressing to hear about the death
of a famous actor.

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:08.900
Their passings can evoke the feeling of an
end to an era, as once-vital, charismatic

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.900
performers head offstage for good after a
final bow.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.000
These actors made us laugh, made us cry, and
made us feel - and many of them seem like

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.200
they're gone too soon.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.340
Let's remember some of the actors that we've
lost in 2018.

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.340
Verne Troyer

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.740
Verne Troyer, best known as the actor who
plays Mini-Me in the Austin Powers movies,

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.540
died of alcohol poisoning on April 21 at the
age of 49.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:38.320
Troyer, who stood at 2 feet, 8 inches, was
born with achondroplasia dwarfism - a condition

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.260
that often results in undersized arms and
legs.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:45.840
He grew up in an Amish community in Michigan
and achieved fame when he debuted opposite

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.420
Mike Myers in 1999's Austin Powers: The Spy
Who Shagged Me.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.540
Troyer had more than 50 acting credits to
his name, with roles including Griphook in

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:00.180
Harry Potter and the Sorcerer's Stone and
Coach Punch Cherkov in The Love Guru.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:04.960
A message announcing his death posted to his
Facebook page said, "Verne was an extremely

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.240
caring individual.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.420
He wanted to make everyone smile, be happy,
and laugh."

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.980
Stefán Karl Stefánsson

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:17.759
Stefán Karl Stefánsson, an Icelandic actor
most known for his role as Robbie Rotten on

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:23.619
the TV series Lazy Town, died on August 21
among his friends and family following a years-long

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.360
battle with cancer of the bile duct.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.020
He was 43 years old.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.180
As an actor, Stefánsson was most readily
identifiable with his work on the popular

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.549
children's series, which aired in more than
100 countries, and on which he starred for

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:37.680
more than ten years.

00:01:37.789 --> 00:01:42.160
A huge portion of his notoriety came from
a popular meme that sprung from the series,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.240
based on the exuberant musical number "We
Are Number One."

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.280
"We are number one, hey!"

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:56.000
Whatever its ironic roots may have been, the
appreciation for Stefánsson grew deeply in

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:01.060
online meme communities over the years, and
he showed much appreciation to fans in return.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.260
He is survived by his wife and four children
- and he is forever number one.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.480
Jackson Odell

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.720
Jackson Odell, an actor, musician, and songwriter,
was found dead in his home on June 8th, according

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:19.820
to a report by TMZ. 20 years old, he died
of an accidental overdose of heroin and cocaine.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.860
Odell began his acting career at the age of
11, making his debut on the series Private

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:25.360
Practice.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.180
He landed his first major part in the 2011
feature Judy Moody and the Not Bummer Summer,

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.480
going on to make appearances on iCarly, Modern
Family, and Arrested Development.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:40.780
As an actor, Odell is most known to audiences
for his work on The Goldbergs, where he played

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.800
recurring character Ari Caldwell.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:47.990
In recent years, Odell's work had pivoted
toward a role as a singer-songwriter, with

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:52.670
several of his compositions appearing on the
soundtracks to the 2016 movie Great Plains

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:54.940
and the 2018 release Forever My Girl.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.200
Jon Schnepp

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.379
Jon Schnepp, a writer, director, editor and
animator known for his many contributions

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:07.660
to Adult Swim programming, died on July 19th
from complications following a massive stroke.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:08.680
He was 51.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.040
Primarily known for his work on Metalocalypse,
Schnepp also occasionally contributed to that

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:18.180
series and others as a voice actor, with his
other appearances including Aqua Teen Hunger

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.260
Force and Space Ghost Coast 2 Coast.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:25.340
He directed several documentaries, including
The Death of "Superman Lives": What Happened?.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:30.560
He was also an on-camera personality for entertainment
website Collider, appearing as a panelist

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.620
on Collider Movie Talk and hosting Collider
Heroes.

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:35.620
Anthony Bourdain

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:40.540
Anthony Bourdain, a writer, presenter, producer
and performer who helped shape the way we

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:45.920
see international food culture, took his own
life on June 8th in France during the production

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.300
of his CNN travelogue series Parts Unknown.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:49.480
He was 61.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.600
Bourdain rose to prominence in 2000 on the
back of his bestselling book Kitchen Confidential:

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:56.320
Adventures in the Culinary Underbelly.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.379
His unapologetic attitude and confessional
style helped endear him to a wide audience,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:04.560
with the book being adapted into a television
series in 2005.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.080
That show, also called Kitchen Confidential,
starred Bradley Cooper as a "bad boy chef"

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.380
based on Bourdain himself.

00:04:11.709 --> 00:04:15.980
Bourdain grew to be a charismatic presence
on the international food scene as he began

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.959
appearing on television as the host of several
shows, including A Cook's Tour, No Reservations,

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:21.860
and The Layover.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:27.660
His notoriety also led to winking cameo appearances,
with Bourdain appearing as himself in the

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.460
2015 film The Big Short.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.340
He also lent his voice to characters inspired
by him in the animated series Archer and Sanjay

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:34.860
and Craig.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.280
He was decorated with awards from the entertainment
industry for his work, earning five Emmys

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:40.000
and a Peabody Award.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.520
Pamela Gidley

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:48.210
Model-turned-actress Pamela Gidley passed
away at the age of 52 on April 16th, dying

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.760
peacefully of natural causes.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:55.640
Gidley's debut role was in the 1986 skateboarding
movie Thrashin', in which she appeared alongside

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:00.240
Josh Brolin and childhood friend Sherilyn
Fenn, who would later play Audrey on the series

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:00.980
Twin Peaks.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.900
Early in her career, Gidley appeared in the
cult sci-fi comedy movie Cherry 2000, playing

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.800
a lifelike android designed to be "the perfect
mate."

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:14.060
She went on to have her own notable role in
the 1992 Twin Peaks prequel feature Fire Walk

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.380
With Me, appearing as the first victim of
the killer who would later haunt the series.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.680
She also had a regular role on the buddy cop
series Angel Street and starred on the mystery

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:27.620
series Strange Luck. with later roles including
work on The Pretender, CSI, and Skin.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:33.110
Her last credited role is Twin Peaks: The
Missing Pieces, a long-lost compilation of

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:35.940
extended and deleted scenes from Fire Walk
With Me.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.140
Mickey Jones

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:43.360
Mickey Jones, a musician and character actor
known for his roles in Home Improvement and

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:46.840
Justified, died on February 7th at the age
of 76.

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.340
Jones worked as an entertainer since his teenage
years, working as a drummer with the likes

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.400
of Trini Lopez, Bob Dylan, and Kenny Rogers
and The First Edition.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:06:01.140
He began pivoting to an acting career in the
late '60s; by 1976, Jones was a full-time

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:05.420
actor, and went on to appear in a number of
TV shows and movies including The Rockford

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.920
Files, Charlie's Angels, The Dukes of Hazzard,
and The Incredible Hulk.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:13.480
Jones' regular role on Home Improvement as
construction worker Pete Bilker made use of

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:17.500
both his talents, with the character playing
the drums in a company band.

00:06:17.789 --> 00:06:22.400
He also had a recurring role in Justified,
playing marijuana distributor Rodney "Hot

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:23.080
Rod" Dunham.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.440
His film roles include Tin Cup, Total Recall,
and Sling Blade, in which he also put his

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:29.660
drumming talents to use.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:35.540
His final roles include the NBC comedy series
Growing Up Fisher and a 2015 appearance in

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:37.660
the Adult Swim comedy Newsreaders.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:39.540
Leon White

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:46.200
Professional wrestler Leon Allen White, better
known as Vader, died on June 18 from complications

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.720
from pneumonia after a years-long battle with
congestive heart failure.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:51.040
He was 63.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.919
Also known as Baby Bull and Big Van Vader,
White rose to prominence in wrestling during

00:06:55.919 --> 00:07:00.840
the early '90s, coming into his own as a formidable
heel who entered rings with a distinctive

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.340
and imposing dark helmet.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:06.860
Like many wrestlers, White occasionally took
his charismatic performing talents to more

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:10.940
mainstream productions, appearing on episodes
of Baywatch and Boy Meets World.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:16.700
His character also appeared in a WCW-themed
comic book series published by Marvel Comics.

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:21.710
In November 2016, White was diagnosed with
congestive heart failure and given less than

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:22.720
two years to live.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:27.970
He faced the development as only a professional
wrestler would, selling T-shirts with slogans

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:32.520
such as "Big Vader Just Won't Die" and "2
Years to Live My Ass."

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:36.650
He is survived by two children, including
the second-generation professional wrestler

00:07:36.650 --> 00:07:40.760
Jesse White, who is also known by the stage
name of Jake Carter.

00:07:41.840 --> 00:07:42.880
Stanley Anderson

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.680
Stage and screen actor Stanley Anderson died
on June 24th at his home in Santa Rosa, California

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:53.480
at the age of 78, six weeks after receiving
a diagnosis of brain cancer.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:56.620
Anderson was born in 1939 in Billings, Montana.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.870
While he would go on to leave his mark on
a number of TV shows and movies, he didn't

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:05.560
segue into onscreen work until he was already
a practiced veteran of the stage.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:10.840
His career on screen began in the 1990s with
appearances on such series as The Drew Carey

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:13.320
Show, The Practice, and NYPD Blue.

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:17.620
Among his more notable performances was his
role as the judge in the widely seen series

00:08:17.629 --> 00:08:23.490
finale of Seinfeld, and as an early adversary
of the Green Goblin in the Sam Raimi-directed

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:24.060
Spider-Man.

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:28.780
In addition to his work as a performer, Anderson
was also a voiceover talent, reading scripts

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:34.100
for documentaries on National Geographic,
Discovery, TLC, PBS, and the History Channel.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:39.440
He also lent his voice to nationwide advertisements
for Democratic party candidates and causes.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:43.630
According to his family, quote, "He was most
proud, ultimately, of the part he played in

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:44.280
politics."

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:46.520
Robert Mandan

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:51.620
Robert Mandan, an actor who held a consistent
presence on television for more than four

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:56.440
decades, died on April 29th in Los Angeles
following a long period of illness.

00:08:56.700 --> 00:08:57.940
He was 86 years old.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.900
Born in Missouri in 1932, Mandan appeared
on a number of soap operas throughout his

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:06.940
career, such as General Hospital, Santa Barbara,
and Days of Our Lives.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:12.860
But his most acclaimed role was on the parody
sitcom Soap, which ran from 1977 to 1981.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:18.170
Beyond Soap, Mandan appeared on the sitcom
Three's Company, continuing on to that show's

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:20.360
1984 spin-off Three's a Crowd.

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:24.600
His other notable television roles include
turns on Married… With Children, Sanford

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:30.100
and Son, and Star Trek: Deep Space Nine - among
many, many more over a long and robust career.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:32.360
Eunice Gayson

00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:37.440
Eunice Gayson, who helped establish a cinematic
tradition as the first "Bond girl" of the

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.680
James Bond film series, died on June 8th at
the age of 90.

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:47.200
Gayson's most well-known role remains her
turn as Sylvia Trench in Dr. No and From Russia

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:51.720
with Love, from which she became connected
to one of the all-time classic lines in cinema.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:57.520
Introducing herself as "Trench, Sylvia Trench,"
her character prompted the immortal introduction

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:01.580
of Sean Connery's MI6 agent, "Bond, James
Bond."

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:06.040
Her performance as a capable foil for Bond
established a template for the movies that's

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:07.140
still in use today.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:12.740
After her turn in the Bond series, Gayson
continued to act on-screen until 1972, after

00:10:12.760 --> 00:10:15.180
which she exclusively performed on-stage.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.910
She is survived by a daughter, Kate, who followed
up on her mother's James Bond legacy with

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:22.440
an appearance in the 1995 film GoldenEye.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:28.420
If you or anyone you know is having suicidal
thoughts, please call or chat online with

00:10:28.420 --> 00:10:33.110
the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline at
1-800-273-TALK.

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:36.620
That's 1-800-273-8255.

00:10:37.060 --> 00:10:41.320
If you or someone you know is struggling with
addiction, please call the Substance Abuse

00:10:41.320 --> 00:10:47.480
and Mental Health Administration's 24/7 National
Helpline at 1-800-662-HELP.

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:51.790
That number again is 1-800-662-4357.

